      "findings": [
        {
          "key": "itsdangerous-control|resource_exhaustion|src/itsdangerous/url_safe.py|URLSafeSerializerMixin.load_payload|44",
          "matched": false,
          "verdict": null,
          "class": "resource_exhaustion",
          "severity": "medium",
          "file": "src/itsdangerous/url_safe.py",
          "lines": [
            44,
            51
          ],
          "symbol": "URLSafeSerializerMixin.load_payload",
          "title": "Unauthenticated memory exhaustion via unbounded zlib decompression of tokens",
          "description": "Entry: src/itsdangerous/url_safe.py:46 (URLSafeSerializerMixin.load_payload), reached from src/itsdangerous/serializer.py:392 (Serializer._loads_unsafe_impl -> load_payload(e.payload)) and from the documented pattern at docs/serializer.rst:58 where the application calls s.load_payload(e.payload) after catching BadSignature.\nAttacker: An unauthenticated caller who controls a token (cookie, URL parameter, form field) but holds no valid signature, crossing the boundary that says only payloads with a verified signature may be unserialized.\nPath: the caller-supplied value is passed to loads_unsafe/load_payload; in _loads_unsafe_impl the signature check raises BadSignature, e.payload (the attacker's raw value, with no valid signature) is extracted, and it is passed to self.load_payload at serializer.py:392. URLSafeSerializerMixin.load_payload at url_safe.py:32-34 sees the leading b'.', strips it and sets decompress = True, base64-decodes at line 37, and at line 46 calls zlib.decompress(json) on the attacker's compressed bytes.\nWhy nothing stops it: there is no signature verification on this branch (it is precisely the failure branch), no output-size cap on zlib.decompress (no max_length argument), and no limit/MAX_/max_length constant anywhere in the package (grep for max_length/limit/MAX_ returns nothing). The compressed input is small and passes any HTTP body-size limit, so a body limit on the request does not bound the decompressed output. The zlib-decompressed bytes are then handed to json.loads, so the allocation happens before any content check.\nImpact: A roughly 1 KB token such as b'.' + base64(zlib-compressed zeros) inflates to gigabytes in a single request; a handful of concurrent requests exhausts process memory and can OOM-kill or stall the server. Because the application is expected to call load_payload/loads_unsafe on invalid signatures (the documented debugging path), any app following that guidance exposes this to unauthenticated callers.\nFix: Decompress with a bounded output size, e.g. use a zlib.decompressobj() and read at most a configured cap (decompress(data, max_length) / decompressor.decompress(json, MAX_PAYLOAD_SIZE) plus a check on the remaining unconsumed data), raising BadPayload when the cap is exceeded; additionally cap the accepted token length before decoding, and document that load_payload/loads_unsafe must not be invoked on attacker-controlled input without such limits."
        }
      ],
